<title>SRI LANKA: Rain likely to cut Sri Lankan rubber output.</title>
<headline>Rain likely to cut Sri Lankan rubber output.</headline>
<dateline>COLOMBO 1996-10-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Sri Lankan rubber output is expected to be cut sharply in the near future if current rains continue, brokers said on Thursday.</p>
<p>&quot;In time to come, there will be a severe rubber shortage,&quot; Upali Bandaranayake of brokers Forbes &amp; Walker said. &quot;In September alone there were 24 days of non-tapping due to rain in growing areas.&quot;</p>
<p>Rain affects rubber tapping and results in the production of lower quality rubber.</p>
<p>For the past month, inter-monsoonal showers have been lashing the country. The northeast monsoon normally begins in mid-November after May-June southwest monsoon.</p>
<p>--Colombo newsroom (tel 941-434319)</p>
</text>
